# uping — Debian/Ubuntu package notes
#
# This package installs a system deployment in the standard directories. The
# `~/uping` checkout from the README keeps working and is unaffected; use one or
# the other for a given port.

## Install

    sudo dpkg -i uping_0.4.0-1_amd64.deb

That is the whole install. The post-installation script:

* creates the unprivileged system user and group `uping`;
* creates `/var/lib/uping`, owned by that user (the writable state);
* installs a working default `/etc/uping/config.toml` when none exists;
* enables and starts `uping.service`.

The default is deliberately harmless: it listens on `127.0.0.1:8080` and
`127.0.0.1:8443`, obtains a `localhost` certificate from uping's own CA, and
points at a placeholder upstream. No network access, no ACME, no public port.
The service starts and the TLS handshake works; requests answer `502` until an
upstream is configured. Check it:

    man uping
    systemctl status uping
    curl -sS --cacert /var/lib/uping/internal/ca/root.crt \
      -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' https://localhost:8443/

## Moving to your domains

Edit the configuration, validate it as the service user, then restart:

    sudo editor /etc/uping/config.toml
    sudo -u uping /usr/bin/uping --check /etc/uping/config.toml
    sudo systemctl restart uping

`/usr/share/uping/config.toml.example` is a commented template with public
ACME, private local-CA and static shapes. Every relative path in the file is
resolved against `/etc/uping`, which the service user cannot write, so anything
uping generates belongs under `/var/lib/uping`.

The local CA signs one zone, `[internal] domain`, and nothing outside it:

    [internal]
    domain     = "internal.rao-nagos.pf"       # the only zone it signs
    ca_dir     = "/var/lib/uping/internal/ca"   # root + intermediate pairs
    cert_dir   = "/var/lib/uping/internal"      # leaves, each with its chain
    serve_root = "https://internal.rao-nagos.pf:9000"

The root signs an intermediate, the intermediate signs the leaves; clients
install only `root.crt`, and after the first start `root.key` can be moved
offline. `serve_root` serves `root.crt` over TLS at that URL, with a certificate
from the same CA — so the first fetch needs `curl -k`, and the file it downloads is what
makes the URL verify from then on:

    sudo curl -k https://internal.rao-nagos.pf:9000/root.crt \
        -o /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/uping-internal.crt
    sudo update-ca-certificates

A `keys = "internal"` name outside the zone is refused when the configuration
loads.

Listening on `:80`/`:443` needs no extra step: the unit carries
`AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`, with a capability bounding set that
holds nothing else. There is no `setcap` to re-apply after an upgrade.

## Paths

    /usr/bin/uping                                  the binary
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/uping.service           the system unit
    /usr/share/man/man8/uping.8.gz                  man uping
    /usr/share/uping/config.toml.default            the installed default
    /usr/share/uping/config.toml.example            commented template
    /usr/share/doc/uping/                           README.md, README.Debian, copyright
    /etc/uping/config.toml                          your configuration
    /var/lib/uping/                                 CA, leaves, writable state

## Upgrades and removal

* `/etc/uping/config.toml` is written only when it does not exist. An upgrade
  never touches it, and there is no conffile prompt to answer.
* `/var/lib/uping` holds the local CA (`root.key`) and the leaves. `apt purge`
  leaves it in place on purpose: deleting the CA would silently untrust every
  client that installed its root. `README.md` documents how to make a new one if
  you really mean it.
* A removed package stops the service. Reinstalling does not re-enable a
  service you disabled.
